#
# System properties for y2q (Galaxy S20+ 5G, SM-G9860, SM8250)
#

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IMS bring-up overrides
#
# These force the telephony framework to advertise VoLTE / VT / VoWiFi as
# available regardless of what the carrier reports.  They exist so that the
# UI toggles and ImsResolver come up while bringing the userspace IMS stack
# (packages/apps/PhhIms) online, and are NOT a substitute for correct
# CarrierConfig - see overlay/packages/apps/CarrierConfig/res/xml/vendor.xml.
#
# They MUST be written as integers: system/sepolicy/private/property_contexts
# labels persist.dbg.{volte,vt,wfc}_avail_ovr as "exact int", so a value of
# "true" is rejected by init and the override silently does nothing.
#
# Only the three properties that actually exist in AOSP are set.  The upstream
# PhhIms README also suggests persist.dbg.allow_ims_off, but there is no such
# property anywhere in this tree's property_contexts (a grep for persist.dbg
# returns exactly the twelve volte/vt/wfc *_ovr entries), so it is omitted
# rather than shipped as dead config.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
persist.dbg.volte_avail_ovr=1
persist.dbg.vt_avail_ovr=0
persist.dbg.wfc_avail_ovr=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bluetooth HCI timeouts - REQUIRED, not tuning
#
# com.android.bluetooth aborts itself roughly five seconds after a connection is
# started from the Bluetooth settings screen, which drops the connection it just
# established.  Measured on the device with an HCI snoop capture:
#
#   14:24:00.972  CMD  Create_Connection (0x0405)
#   14:24:02.972  the HCI timeout expires            <- exactly 2000 ms later
#   14:24:02.987  EVT  Command Status for 0x0405     <- arrives 15 ms TOO LATE
#   14:24:03.848  EVT  Connection_Complete           <- the link does come up
#   14:24:07.980  Fatal signal 6 (SIGABRT)           <- abort_after_time_out
#
# The defaults are
#     HciLayer::kHciTimeoutMs        = 2000 ms   (hci_layer.h:147)
#     HciLayer::kHciTimeoutRestartMs = 5000 ms   (hci_layer.h:148)
# raised by system/gd/hci/hci_layer.cc:73-84 from the properties below.  Both are
# read ONCE into a function-local static, so they only take effect on a Bluetooth
# restart and cannot be changed with setprop after the stack is up.
#
# 2000 ms is simply too tight for this controller.  libbt-vendor puts the BCM4375
# into low power after LPM_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MULTIPLE * host_stack_idle_threshold * 300
# = 1500 ms of inactivity, and the chip needs roughly half a second to answer
# again once BT_WAKE is asserted - so the first command after an idle period lands
# at ~2015 ms and misses the deadline by a hair.  The stack then flushes its
# command queue, asks for controller debug info, and calls log::fatal():
#
#   bt_gd_shim: StartEverything: init_status == 1        (1 = future timeout)
#   Abort message: 'system/gd/hci/hci_layer.cc:96 abort_after_time_out:
#       Done waiting for debug information after HCI timeout
#       (CREATE_CONNECTION(0x0405)) for 5000ms'
#
# Raising the HCI timeout is the fix.  The other two properties have to move with
# it: bluetooth.gd.start_timeout (3000 ms default, system/main/shim/stack.cc:399)
# bounds the same controller handshake during start-up, so leaving it at 3000 ms
# while the HCI timeout is 8000 ms makes Bluetooth fail to start instead -
# "init_status == 1" on every enable.
#
# These are deliberately world-readable platform properties rather than
# persist.*: the Bluetooth process reads them at start-up and nothing needs to
# change them at runtime.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
bluetooth.hci.timeout_milliseconds=8000
bluetooth.hci.restart_timeout_milliseconds=15000
bluetooth.gd.start_timeout=20000
